  "content": "# Bounded LRU Cache in Python: A Complete Tutorial\n\nAn **LRU (Least Recently Used)** cache evicts the least recently used item when the cache reaches its capacity. In Python, `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation because it maintains insertion order and supports moving keys to the end in O(1) time, which is exactly what LRU semantics require.\n\nBelow is a complete, production-ready implementation with thorough explanation, edge cases, tests, a worked example, and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Core Idea\n\nIn an LRU cache:\n- **Most recent** items are at the **end** of the order.\n- **Least recent** items are at the **beginning** of the order.\n- On a `get`, we promote the accessed key to the most-recent position.\n- On a `put`, if the cache is full and the key doesn't already exist, we evict the first (least recently used) entry.\n\n`OrderedDict` gives us:\n- `move_to_end(key)` \u2014 promotes a key to the end (most recent).\n- `popitem(last=False)` \u2014 removes and returns the first (least recently used) item.\n\nBoth operations are O(1) in the average case (and amortized O(1) in the worst case for the underlying hash table).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom typing import Any, Optional, Callable, TypeVar\n\nK = TypeVar('K')\nV = TypeVar('V')\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU (Least Recently Used) cache backed by an OrderedDict.\n\n    - Accessing or inserting a key marks it as \"most recently used.\"\n    - When capacity is reached, the least recently used key is evicted.\n    - If a key already exists, its value is updated and it is promoted\n      to most recently used.\n\n    All public operations run in O(1) average time.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache.\n\n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n                       Must